WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.660
Should we speak in a
language before we're ready?

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:04.890
That's the subject today.

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:06.660
It comes up all the time.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.540
People are afraid; they're intimidated.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:13.470
They know that they speak with mistakes or
with a poor accent, so they don't speak.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.540
"I would like to buy a hamburger.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.360
I would like to buy a hamburger.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:22.260
And of course you know that
I'm very much into input.

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:26.520
I believe that you can continue to
build yourself up, get used to the

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.810
language, develop comprehension
skills, acquire a passive vocabulary.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:35.250
All of this is making you
better prepared for speaking.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:39.720
But there comes a point when you have
accumulated a lot of words and you've

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:44.490
done a lot of listening, and at some
point you have to jump in and get wet.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.060
And this was very much
my situation with Polish.

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:52.620
Eight years ago I did three months of
Polish, and then I didn't do anything.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:56.310
And two months prior to going to
Poland, I spent a lot of time

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:58.050
listening to and reading polish.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:05.070
Probably my comprehension ability—and
certainly my vocabulary—in Polish grew.

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:08.880
I learned more new words during
this two-month period than I have

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:14.580
forgotten over the past eight years.
But probably my speaking ability

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:16.940
was better eight years ago.

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:17.450
Why?

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:20.450
Because I spoke more eight years ago.

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:24.860
I had I don't know how many online
sessions during a three-month period

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:31.940
with my Polish tutor. And typically in
languages where the alphabet is the Latin

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:37.160
alphabet, or it's a familiar alphabet,
after a month or so, six weeks, with

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:40.640
the [LingQ] Mini Stories, I feel I'm ready
to start to struggle in the language.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:44.300
And if I speak two, three times a
week, you know, the more you speak,

00:01:44.895 --> 00:01:48.615
the better you get. As I always say, "To
speak well, you have to speak a lot."

00:01:48.735 --> 00:01:53.595
So if you look back at my video that I
did eight years ago, I don't speak so bad.

00:01:53.595 --> 00:01:57.134
I don't speak well, but
relative to the amount of time I

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:59.055
put in, it wasn't all that bad.

00:01:59.595 --> 00:02:05.445
This time traveling with my wife
in Poland, I had the odd semi-

00:02:05.445 --> 00:02:10.155
conversation with a taxi driver
or flight attendants on the

00:02:10.155 --> 00:02:12.255
airplane on LOT [Polish Airlines] coming over.

00:02:12.555 --> 00:02:14.204
Not very successful.

00:02:14.415 --> 00:02:18.375
And then all of a sudden I was put
in a situation where I was gonna

00:02:18.375 --> 00:02:23.745
have an interview in Polish, and
I'm going to show you this video.

00:02:24.165 --> 00:02:25.035
At the end of this video.

00:02:25.035 --> 00:02:27.495
You can see me speaking in Polish.

00:02:27.915 --> 00:02:31.725
And you can also follow a link
to Easy Polish where the whole

00:02:31.725 --> 00:02:33.255
interview was put up.

00:02:33.765 --> 00:02:34.935
And you will see.

00:02:35.325 --> 00:02:39.075
Comments from people that are
mostly somewhat critical of my

00:02:39.075 --> 00:02:42.465
polish, saying that it's kind of a
mixture of Polish, Czech, Ukrainian,

00:02:42.465 --> 00:02:43.665
and Russian. Which is true.

00:02:43.785 --> 00:02:46.125
People saying that "Steve
doesn't speak any languages.

00:02:46.125 --> 00:02:49.065
He's always all over the
internet pretending to be a polyglot."

00:02:49.505 --> 00:02:50.805
A variety of negative comments.

00:02:50.835 --> 00:02:57.315
Others are encouraging, but the point
is, I knew going in that I was not

00:02:57.315 --> 00:03:01.665
going to speak very well in Polish.
However, tt was still, from my

00:03:01.665 --> 00:03:04.845
perspective, a very positive experience.

00:03:04.905 --> 00:03:08.895
I actually communicated! I mean,
if you follow the video, it's a bit

00:03:08.895 --> 00:03:12.795
painful. I throw in some English
and various other Slavic languages.

00:03:12.795 --> 00:03:17.175
I do my best, but we are communicating!
The person interviewing me

00:03:17.535 --> 00:03:19.125
understands what I'm saying.

00:03:19.425 --> 00:03:21.825
I understand what she's
saying most of the time.

00:03:21.945 --> 00:03:23.325
So there is communication.

00:03:23.655 --> 00:03:28.095
If I were to do that, say
night after night in Poland,

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:31.530
Or frequently, or three
times a week online.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:36.030
My speaking ability would improve a
lot because I have this background

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:40.620
of passive vocabulary. But the fact
that I'm gonna make mistakes, the fact

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:45.420
that I'm somewhat, yeah, I would say
intimidated by the prospect of being

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:48.420
interviewed in Polish when I know I'm
not gonna do well, didn't prevent me

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.250
from doing it, and I'm glad I did it.

00:03:50.549 --> 00:03:54.209
So the initial question is,
"Should we speak before we're ready?"

00:03:54.209 --> 00:03:56.940
Well, the answer
is "You're never ready!"

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:02.310
We are never as good in the
language as we would like to be if

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:04.800
the objective is to perform well.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:09.210
But if the objective is to
communicate, like if I'm in a situation,

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:10.980
it's going to be in Polish.

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:16.260
We have to communicate. I communicate.
I grab whatever I have, whatever I can

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:20.220
remember of Polish, whatever comes to
my mind that's in a Slavic language.

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:26.790
I throw in the odd English word and we
communicate. And what I hear from her is

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:32.340
pure, relevant, high-resonance content.
So that it's a very good experience.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:33.660
So, I'll leave you the video.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:36.270
You can have a look yourselves and you
can send all the critical comments you

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:40.980
want, but I don't mind because even if
I spend another six months working on my

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:45.930
Polish, improving my Polish, it'll still
not be as good as I would like it to be.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:49.560
I think in language learning we're never
quite as good as we would like to be.

00:04:49.715 --> 00:04:52.385
And yet we still have to go
there and use the language.

00:04:52.535 --> 00:04:56.045
So my answer is, you know, should
we speak before we're ready?

00:04:56.045 --> 00:04:56.705
Yes.

00:04:56.705 --> 00:04:58.445
Because we're never really ready.

00:04:58.685 --> 00:05:03.275
But we should nevertheless, you know,
try to prepare as much as we can,

00:05:03.275 --> 00:05:05.135
focusing on our input activities.

00:05:05.285 --> 00:05:06.215
So there you have it.

00:05:07.055 --> 00:05:08.585
Speak when you feel like it.

00:05:09.185 --> 00:05:09.725
Bye for now.

